{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-161205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/658f58654e8791f15fe6c2a9c719de3b02aa1c3912b4b6dc7b4e2fe2d2cdda62.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/05664db64ac6595ff0caec7daeec20cb3508d5c18a5266aa126295e3aa040233.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-3)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-z)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,(3-0)\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-3\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =-3{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-3{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-6\\pi \\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-2\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161205" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7176b3402370922ac198b9d74f0e6b226a5ed28b19a1c178cdb8623c493df15e"} {"question" "q-161205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2\\pi "] ["math" "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{\\pi }{4}"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{3}"] ["math" "\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161205" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-2\\pi "] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/c8c7f186ba8769231e9245e0887501f666e56c317fd4f546550202956365b5dc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=2$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}⟨x+yz,\\,y-xz,\\,z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}(x(x+yz)+y(y-xz)+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+xyz+{y}^{2}-xyz+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{2})^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7176b3402370922ac198b9d74f0e6b226a5ed28b19a1c178cdb8623c493df15e"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "16\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "4\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] ["math" "8\\pi "] ["math" "8\\pi \\sqrt{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161235" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8\\pi \\sqrt{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/1ee9e32dc5dbe7ba1222a5448ce9ae1227a461a40a36f13e337fce7990263408.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $4$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(xz+0-xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7176b3402370922ac198b9d74f0e6b226a5ed28b19a1c178cdb8623c493df15e"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "12\\pi "] ["math" "16\\pi "] ["math" "2\\pi "] ["math" "4\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-161237" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/7ca9996f6d0185d99752ccf5201b5300e0e5149ad674bb9676cf8c716b71a171.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/320e0c3333aa59f4e856d7d869d6fece09b178ac1abb3acd402621977df9b496.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2(3)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{3}{(25-{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\pi (64-125) \\\\  & =-4\\pi (-61) \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,2(0)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =244\\pi +0 \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7176b3402370922ac198b9d74f0e6b226a5ed28b19a1c178cdb8623c493df15e"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "128\\pi "] ["math" "162\\pi "] ["math" "244\\pi "] ["math" "64\\pi "] ["math" "81\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162543" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "244\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/dedd58b5eeeba8b2874c740543132026f95157597dd12ad31fbaa476a3d77c2e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/assets/9ffcdd701a8ac6b12cd62773e1cabfd2ce95f9a809e18d087c4bf4f4a22eadb5.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,({4}^{2})(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{r}^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(6-{r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[3{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =32\\pi (\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,{0}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =216\\pi +0 \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "7176b3402370922ac198b9d74f0e6b226a5ed28b19a1c178cdb8623c493df15e"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "128\\pi "] ["math" "216\\pi "] ["math" "243\\pi "] ["math" "64\\pi "] ["math" "81\\pi "]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"} {"question" "q-162547" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "216\\pi "] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14079020/state.json" "file_sha256" "0d9ca115cd2c26b366588b413f719831e847d0def7301ad724b4bb8c6ba45462"}] "usage" {} "basis" 2820 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
